### Key Update for OSCORE (KUDOS)
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-core-oscore-key-update`
* **Presented by:** Rikard Höglund
* **Slides:** [Slides KUDOS CoRE IETF 127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-slides-kudos-core-ietf-127-00)
* Updated references from EDHOC to LAKE. Incorporated lessons learned from interop testing between RISE (Java) and University of Murcia (C) implementations, explicitly specifying short-lexicographic ordering in `update_ctx` and 1-byte length prefixes for variable-length fields in `KUDOS-Expand-Label`.
* Extended interop testing was successfully completed for forward-secrecy (FS) and no-FS modes.
* Relationship to `draft-ietf-core-oscore-id-update` was noted; KUDOS will undergo a minor editorial revision before requesting WGLC.

### OSCORE-Capable Proxies
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-core-oscore-capable-proxies`
* **Presented by:** Rikard Höglund
* **Slides:** [Slides OSCORE Capable Proxies CoRE IETF 127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-slides-oscore-capable-proxies-core-ietf-127-00)
* Refined step 4 of the option protection escalation procedure (Class U to Class E) to prevent hiding necessary options from downstream proxies in a chain.
* Added Section 2.7 defining replay protection and out-of-order response handling for proxy OSCORE legs.
* Introduced two DNS SVCB/SVP SvcParamKeys (`oscore-with-proxy` and `nested-oscore`) for proxy capabilities discovery.
* The draft is functionally complete; chairs noted that WGLC will be scheduled sequentially alongside other pending WGLCs.

### Using KEMs in Group OSCORE
* **Draft:** `draft-tiloca-core-oscore-zeroconf-pqc`
* **Presented by:** Marco Tiloca
* **Slides:** [Using KEMs in Group OSCORE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-using-kems-in-group-oscore-00)
* Addresses post-quantum security for pairwise key agreement in Group OSCORE (`draft-ietf-core-cacheable-oscore`) by replacing Diffie-Hellman static-static key agreement with Key Encapsulation Mechanisms (KEMs, e.g., ML-KEM).
* The Group Manager acts as a relayer for uploading/retrieving KEM public keys and encapsulated shared secrets between group members.
* Updated terminology to alignment with HPKE ("encapsulated shared secret"), added security considerations regarding randomness and ML-KEM, and outlined future post-quantum Diffie-Hellman primitive integration.
* The author requested a Working Group Adoption Call.

### Stand-in KID and Encrypted Partial IV in the OSCORE Option
* **Draft:** `draft-tiloca-core-oscore-missing-security`
* **Presented by:** Marco Tiloca
* **Slides:** [Stand-in KID and Encrypted Partial IV in the OSCORE Option](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-stand-in-kid-and-encrypted-partial-iv-in-the-oscore-option-00)
* Proposes lightweight obfuscation of Partial IV and KID fields in the OSCORE option to protect against traffic analysis and endpoint tracking without signaling on the wire.
* Fixed the obfuscation key length to 16 bytes and generalized byte sampling from the COSE ciphertext payload.
* Clarified recipient context exploration to minimize false positive overhead and addressed timing side-channel mitigations.

### Oblivious CoAP
* **Draft:** `draft-brachmann-core-oblivious-coap`
* **Presented by:** Martina Brachmann
* **Slides:** [Slides Oblivious CoAP IETF 127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-slides-oblivious-coap-ietf-127-00)
* Adapts Oblivious HTTP (RFC 9548) to CoAP using HPKE to decouple client identities from origin servers via forward proxies and gateway resources.
* Defines an critical `O-CoAP` option, message format classifications (Class U/I/E), payload structures, and CBOR-based key configuration formats.
* Proof-of-concept request/response, block-wise, and observe functionality were implemented during the hackathon.
* **Discussion:** Christian Amsüss questioned whether Group OSCORE features could achieve similar decoupling with less code/concept duplication. Martina Brachmann and Mike Bishop noted that HPKE alignment with O-HTTP simplifies cross-protocol deployment and explicitly enforces server privacy policies.

### 2.06 No Content
* **Draft:** `draft-bormann-core-206-no-content` (related to `draft-ietf-core-responses`)
* **Presented by:** Carsten Bormann
* **Slides:** [2.06 No Content](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-206-no-content-00)
* Introduces a `2.06 No Content` CoAP response code indicating that a target resource exists but currently lacks a representation, allowing Observe relationships to remain active (e.g., in CoAP PubSub brokers when topic data is cleared).
* This work is an explicit dependency blocking CoAP PubSub, ACE, and LAKE specifications.
* **Discussion:** Christian Amsüss recalled an alternative proposal (`accept-any` updated accept option) from 2019 and suggested evaluating if updating options is less invasive. Carsten Bormann noted both approaches have trade-offs and recommended progressing to an adoption call to resolve the approach.

### CoAP Diagnostic Message Notation
* **Draft:** `draft-bormann-core-coap-diag`
* **Presented by:** Jaime Jiménez, Carsten Bormann
* **Slides:** [CoAP Diagnostic Message Notation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-coap-diagnostic-message-notation-01)
* Standardizes human-readable and machine-parsable diagnostic notation for CoAP requests, responses, options, and payloads across specifications to enable automated syntax checking in toolchains.
* Added support for hex payloads (DNS over CoAP), Observe notification streams, multipart messages, and OSCORE options.
* Discussion focused on handling combined options (Location-Path/Query, Proxy-URI/Scheme) and standardizing hex string formatting (`0x` vs `h'...'` vs raw hex).

### SID File Extensions
* **Draft:** `draft-fernandez-core-sid-extension`
* **Presented by:** Javier Fernández
* **Slides:** [SID File Extensions](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-core-sid-file-extensions-01)
* Extends YANG SID files with YANG data type and list key metadata. This enables constrained CoRECONF nodes to parse and encode CBOR data without needing a full YANG data model parser on the constrained device.
* Real-world trial results and interop experience with external implementations were presented.
* **Poll:** A session poll was conducted on reading the document:
  * **Question:** "Did you read the document?"
  * **Results:** yes: 2, no: 6, no_opinion: 0 (total: 26)
* Carsten Bormann expressed support for compiling YANG models into lightweight device representations and offered to review the document.

